Title: The Tree
Order: 3
Production Code: 1x03

Radio play, Part 3 of 6. Adapts the second half of Part 2 of the novella. Running time about 14 minutes.

INT. THE LISTWELL FARM - SITTING ROOM - NIGHT

AMB: the fire low, the clock, wind at the window

MUSIC: BED IN - the cello, one low note, picking up where the last episode ended

Madison Washington has pulled his chair a little closer to the fire. Mrs. Listwell is leaning toward him like a child at a story.

MADISON
Two weeks ago, after travelling all night, I was overtaken by daybreak, in what seemed to me an almost interminable wood. I deemed it unsafe to go farther, and, as usual, I looked around for a suitable tree in which to spend the day.

MUSIC: BED OUT

EXT. A VIRGINIA WOOD - DAWN - MADISON'S MEMORY

AMB: dawn birds, a heavy dew, a cold wind through bare upper branches

SFX: a man climbing, bark scraping under his hands, boots against the trunk

SFX: a leather strap pulled tight around a bough and buckled

MADISON (V.O.)
I liked one with a bushy top, and found one just to my mind. Up I climbed, and hiding myself as well as I could, I, with this strap, lashed myself to a bough, and flattered myself that I should get a good night's sleep that day.

SFX: distant voices, men's, in a rhythm of talk, coming nearer

MADISON (V.O.) (CONT'D)
But in this I was soon disappointed. I heard the voices of a number of persons, apparently approaching the part of the woods where I was. Upon my word, sir, I dreaded more these human voices than I should have done those of wild beasts.

SFX: axes striking, first one, then a dozen together, ringing across the wood

SFX: a great tree cracking, falling, the ground answering

SFX: men laughing, loud, easy, a whoop at the fall

MADISON (V.O.) (CONT'D)
The men, who were all colored, halted at least a hundred yards from me, and began with their axes, in right good earnest, to attack the trees. The sound of their laughing axes was like the report of as many well-charged pistols.

MUSIC: SOURCE BED IN - a work song among the woodcutters, wordless, in time with the axes

SFX: another tree, falling, the whole wood shuddering

MADISON (V.O.) (CONT'D)
I held my place in the tree till sunset. I saw the men put on their jackets to be off.

AMB: the wood quiet, the song gone, a single axe laid down

MUSIC: SOURCE BED OUT

SFX: an old man's slow footsteps in leaf-mould, stopping under the tree

SFX: a man removing his hat

The Praying Old Man kneels. We hear his knees meet the earth, and a long breath before he speaks.

THE OLD MAN
(praying aloud, quiet and certain)
O thou that hearest the raven's cry, take pity on poor me. O deliver me. O deliver me. In mercy, O God, deliver me from the chains and manifold hardships of slavery.

AMB: wind in the high boughs, a bird settling

THE OLD MAN (CONT'D)
(his voice lifting a little)
With thee, O Father, all things are possible. Thou canst stand and measure the earth. Thou hast beheld and drove asunder the nations. All power is in thy hand. Thou didst say of old, "I have seen the affliction of my people, and am come to deliver them." Oh, look down upon our afflictions, and have mercy upon us.

MUSIC: BED IN - a single sustained voice, a hymn fragment, no words, as if from the tree itself

MADISON (V.O.)
But I cannot repeat his prayer, nor can I give you an idea of its deep pathos. I had given but little attention to religion, and had but little faith in it; yet, as the old man prayed, I felt almost like coming down and kneeling by his side, and mingling my broken complaint with his.

SFX: a strap unbuckled, boots against bark, a man climbing down the trunk

MUSIC: BED OUT

SFX: the old man rising too fast, startled, a hand flung up

MADISON
(low, from the dark)
Don't be afraid.

THE OLD MAN
(a breath, then a long look)
...Child.

MADISON (V.O.)
(quietly)
He seemed a little alarmed at first, but I told him my story, and the good man embraced me in his arms, and assured me of his sympathy.

SFX: an embrace, cloth against cloth, the old man's breath shaking

MADISON (CONT'D)
(to the old man)
I have one dollar. It is all the money I have in the world. Would you buy me some crackers and cheese, and bring me the balance? I will be near here, and come to you on your return, if you will whistle.

THE OLD MAN
(slowly, with a smile)
I will, child. I will whistle.

SFX: the old man's footsteps going away, getting smaller, a short low whistle practised twice

AMB: the wood at dusk, crickets, a sudden loneliness

INT. THE LISTWELL FARM - SITTING ROOM - NIGHT

AMB: the fire, the clock

MADISON (CONT'D)
(to the Listwells)
He was gone only about an hour. Meanwhile, from some cause or other, I know not what, but as you shall see very wisely, I changed my place.

EXT. A VIRGINIA WOOD - NIGHT - MADISON'S MEMORY

AMB: night wood, wind, an owl, black air

SFX: footsteps coming, the old man's slow ones, and many more behind him

SFX: guns carried, a lock clicking, men's low voices

MADISON (V.O.)
On his return I started to meet him; but it seemed as if the shadow of approaching danger fell upon my spirit, and checked my progress. In a very few minutes, closely on the heels of the old man, I distinctly saw fourteen men, with something like guns in their hands.

The Hunters are heard before they are seen: boots, breath, jokes shut off by the old man's silence.

MUSIC: BED IN - low strings, a pulse, a slow dread

SFX: the old man stumbling, a rough hand shoving him forward

THE HUNTER
(an angry voice, sharp)
You lying old villain. You're a thief. You stole that money.

THE OLD MAN
(steady, to the dark)
I did not steal the money. It was given me, as I told you at the store; and if the man who gave it me is not here, it is not my fault.

THE HUNTER
Hush! You lying old rascal; we'll make you smart for it. You shall not leave this spot until you have told where you got that money.

SFX: a shirt torn, a scuffle, a rope thrown over a limb

MADISON (V.O.)
I felt, at the moment, like rushing out in the midst of them; but considering that the old man would be whipped the more for having aided a fugitive slave, and that, perhaps, in the melee he might be killed outright, I disobeyed this impulse.

SFX: a whip, a cracked, wet sound, then a groan

SFX: the old man's cry, once, high and thin, then held back

SFX: the whip. The whip. Voices counting under it, hoarse and jovial.

AMB: wind in the wood, covering and uncovering the sound

MADISON (V.O.) (CONT'D)
My own flesh crept at every blow, and I seem to hear the old man's piteous cries even now.

SFX: thirty-nine blows, we count them, no one speaks, the old man silent after the twentieth

A SECOND HUNTER
(hoarse, offhand)
You'll kill the d----d old scoundrel! You've already whipped a dollar's worth out of him, even if he stole it.

A THIRD HUNTER
(laughing)
O yes. Let him down. He'll never tell us another lie, I'll warrant ye.

SFX: a rope cut, a body falling to the ground, an old man's breath dragging in

MUSIC: BED OUT

SFX: the old man's slow limping footsteps, going away

SFX: the hunters scouring the wood, boots through underbrush, a stick poking into a thicket

MADISON (V.O.)
Round and round they went, turning up the underbrush, and peering about like so many bloodhounds. Two or three times they came within six feet of where I lay. I tell you I held my stick with a firmer grasp than I did in coming up to your house tonight. I expected to level one of them at least.

SFX: a man's boots passing within feet, stopping, moving on

SFX: the hunters' voices dwindling, the wood closing back over them

AMB: a long silence, one night bird

INT. THE LISTWELL FARM - SITTING ROOM - NIGHT

AMB: the fire, the wind, the clock

SFX: Mrs. Listwell, who has been holding her breath, lets it go

MRS. LISTWELL
(furious, whispering)
Oh, the old wretch. He had betrayed you, had he?

MADISON
(gently, correcting)
I think not. I cannot believe that the old man was to blame. He probably went into a store, asked for the articles for which I sent, and presented the bill I gave him; and it is so unusual for slaves in the country to have money that fact, doubtless, excited suspicion.

SFX: a log shifting in the fire

MADISON (CONT'D)
I can easily believe that the truthfulness of the old man's character compelled him to disclose the facts.

MUSIC: BED IN - the cello, soft, in respect

MADISON (CONT'D)
(quiet)
My last dollar was now gone, and you may well suppose I felt the loss of it. But the thought of being once again free to pursue my journey prevented that depression which a sense of destitution causes. So swinging my little bundle on my back, I caught a glimpse of the Great Bear, which ever points the way to my beloved star, and I started again on my journey.

MUSIC: BED OUT

MADISON (CONT'D)
What I lost in money I made up at a hen-roost that same night, upon which I fortunately came.

SFX: Listwell, laughing, a real laugh, short

MRS. LISTWELL
(practical, relieved)
But you didn't eat your food raw? How did you cook it?

MADISON
O no, madam. I had the means of cooking.

SFX: a bundle untied, cloth rustling, a small iron thing set on the table

SFX: a flint struck on a file, a dozen sparks at once, a bright scrape

MADISON (CONT'D)
(a proud little smile in his voice)
I have had this old box more than five years. It is the only property saved from the fire in the dismal swamp. It has done me good service. It has given me the means of broiling many a chicken.

SFX: Mrs. Listwell's soft laugh, a relieved one

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It seemed quite a relief to Mrs. Listwell to know that Madison had, at least, lived upon cooked food.

MUSIC: BED IN - the cello and a little warmth in the strings

INT. THE LISTWELL FARM - THE BEST ROOM - NIGHT

AMB: a small quiet room, the wind softened by thick walls

SFX: a door opened, the soft creak of a bed frame

LISTWELL
(at the door, kindly)
Give yourself no uneasiness. If it cost my farm, I shall see you safely out of the States, and on your way to a land of liberty. You will spend to-morrow with us, and to-morrow night I will take you in my carriage to the Lake. Once upon that, and you are safe.

SFX: Madison standing very still looking at white sheets, the chill of the house around him

MADISON
(a slow incredulous murmur)
What, sir. You don't mean that I shall sleep in that bed?

LISTWELL
(gently)
Oh yes. Oh yes.

SFX: the door closing softly

SFX: Madison sitting on the edge of the bed, then lowering himself carefully to the floor beside it, the boards creaking

NARRATOR (V.O.)
After Mr. Listwell left the room, Madison said he really hesitated whether or not he should lie on the floor; for that was far more comfortable and inviting than any bed to which he had been used.

MUSIC: BED OUT

EXT. THE LISTWELL FARM - YARD - NIGHT

AMB: cold night, snow-light, stars in a cleared sky

SFX: a two-horse wagon being brought round, harness creaking, buffalo robes thrown in

SFX: Madison's boots on the wagon step, Listwell clicking to the horses

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In the evening, Mr. Listwell, after treating Madison to a new suit of winter clothes, and replenishing his exhausted purse with five dollars, all in silver, brought out his two-horse wagon, well provided with buffaloes, and silently started off with him to Cleveland.

SFX: wheels on frozen ruts, harness, a horse's breath in the cold

AMB: a long, long road, a cold wind, a clear sky

MUSIC: BED IN - a slow, patient hand drum, almost a heartbeat

EXT. CLEVELAND - THE WHARF - JUST BEFORE SUNRISE

AMB: gulls, water slapping a hull, a steam whistle far out on the lake, a pale morning

SFX: a steam engine at idle, wood creaking, rope on a cleat

The Admiral lies at the wharf.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
They arrived there without interruption, a few minutes before sunrise the next morning. Fortunately the steamer Admiral lay at the wharf, and was to start for Canada at nine o'clock.

SFX: Listwell's footsteps along planks, a gangway, a quiet voice at the rail

LISTWELL
(low, to The Captain of the Admiral)
Captain, I should like to ask you something, and I hope you will take it as it is meant. You carry passengers to Canada. Might there be room among them for a man who loves liberty?

THE CAPTAIN
(unhurried, warm, a long beat)
A man who loves liberty is welcome on my boat.

SFX: a long exhale, Listwell's, relief

SFX: Madison coming aboard, the gangway, the deck under him

NARRATOR (V.O.)
With usual generosity this true subject of the emancipating queen welcomed Madison, and assured him that he should be safely landed in Canada, free of charge.

SFX: a handshake, a grip held much too long

MADISON
(quiet)
I will not forget you, sir.

LISTWELL
(moved)
Go with God.

SFX: the steamer's whistle, a bell, the paddle-wheel beginning to turn

MUSIC: BED OUT

SFX: the steamer pulling away, the wheel beating the water, gulls

AMB: wind, water, a steam whistle growing smaller

INT. THE LISTWELL FARM - SITTING ROOM - A FEW DAYS LATER

AMB: the fire, the clock, a winter afternoon

SFX: a letter opened, thick paper unfolding

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He went to his home that day with a joy and gratification which knew no bounds. On reaching home, he exclaimed, "He is safe, he is safe, he is safe." A few days after, a letter was received from Madison.

SFX: Listwell clearing his throat, a hand on paper

LISTWELL
(reading, hoarse)
"Windsor, Canada West. December sixteenth, eighteen hundred and forty. My dear Friend, for such you truly are."

MUSIC: SOURCE BED IN - a solo voice, humming a slow spiritual that rises

MADISON (V.O.)
(from a long way off, as if read aloud inside the room)
Madison is out of the woods at last. I nestle in the mane of the British lion, protected by his mighty paw from the talons and the beak of the American eagle. I AM FREE, and breathe an atmosphere too pure for slaves, slave-hunters, or slave-holders.

SFX: Mrs. Listwell, softly, almost crying

MADISON (V.O.) (CONT'D)
My heart is full. As many thanks to you, sir, and to your kind lady, as there are pebbles on the shores of Lake Erie; and may the blessing of God rest upon you both. You will never be forgotten by your profoundly grateful friend, Madison Washington.

SFX: the letter folded, set down on a table

AMB: the fire, the wind, a long peace

MUSIC: SOURCE BED OUT

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But this was not the last that Mr. Listwell would hear of Madison Washington.

MUSIC: STING - the sustained string note, cut off sharply

NARRATOR (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Part Four. The Tavern.

AMB: the fire, then silence
